T10.01|Earnings growth measures an increase in reported profit; earnings quality asks how reliably profit represents sustainable economic performance and future cash generation. Growth from repeat customers differs from growth from one-off gains, optimistic estimates or uncollectible sales.~~Quality assessment combines business understanding, recognition policies, balance movements, cash conversion and disclosures. Weak cash flow is a warning to investigate, not automatic proof that earnings are false.|Sustainable operating earnings: Supported by repeatable business activity.;;Transitory earnings: Gains or effects unlikely to recur.;;Reporting quality: Faithfulness of recognised information to underlying events.|Profit rises from ₹20 to ₹30 lakh, including a new ₹12 asset-sale gain.~~Reported growth is 50%.;;Excluding the supported gain, current earnings are ₹18.;;Compare operating drivers and cash before extrapolating the reported increase.~~Reported earnings growth can mask a decline in recurring performance.~~Normalisation also needs tax and other effects; exclusions must be evidenced and applied consistently.|Reconcile growth sources.;;Test cash and balance-sheet support.;;Separate reporting concerns from business volatility.
T10.02|Aggressive revenue recognition records revenue too early, at excessive amounts or without satisfying governing criteria. Warning signs include unusual period-end sales, extended terms, rising contract assets and poor subsequent collection. The relevant contract and transfer evidence determine whether recognition is valid.~~An unusual transaction can be legitimate. Investigate delivery, acceptance, returns, side agreements and customer credit rather than judging solely by a high growth rate.|Premature timing: Recognition before qualifying performance.;;Inflated amount: Variable consideration or returns inadequately reflected.;;Fictitious transaction: No genuine qualifying economic sale.|A company invoices ₹20 lakh on 31 March but the contract requires delivery and acceptance in April. Assume control has not transferred before acceptance.~~Check the contract rather than invoice date alone.;;Under the stated facts, March revenue is not established.;;Review April acceptance, cancellations and collection evidence.~~Billing does not by itself prove revenue has been earned.~~Different transfer terms can change the conclusion; evidence is essential.|Read actual recognition criteria.;;Inspect cutoff and side agreements.;;Check subsequent delivery, returns and cash.
T10.03|Channel stuffing pushes more product into distributors than sustainable customer demand supports, often through discounts, extended credit or return rights. Round-tripping routes funds or transactions back to their origin to create apparent activity without corresponding independent economic substance.~~These are concerns requiring transaction-level evidence. Distributor inventory and returns can reveal demand pulled forward, while linked cash flows and related counterparties can reveal circularity.|Channel loading: Shipments outrun end demand.;;Side incentives: Terms make headline sales less durable.;;Circular transaction: Reciprocal flows inflate apparent activity.|A producer ships ₹30 lakh to a distributor while end-customer sales are ₹10 and distributor stock rises sharply.~~Distinguish sell-in from sell-through.;;Review payment terms, return rights and distributor capacity.;;Test whether next-period sales and collections support the shipment.~~The ₹20 gap is a prompt to investigate stock accumulation, not proof of fraud.~~Seasonal stocking or a new territory can also explain temporary differences.|Trace independent demand.;;Review terms and later returns.;;Investigate linked counterparties and cash flows.
T10.04|Improper expense deferral moves current costs into assets without meeting recognition criteria, increasing current profit and capital balances. Legitimate capitalisation recognises qualifying benefits and later consumption; forensic analysis asks whether the asset actually exists and is recoverable.~~Compare policies, asset growth and subsequent amortisation. Repeated capitalisation of routine operating expenditure can hide deteriorating unit economics.|Legitimate capitalisation: Cost meets asset criteria.;;Improper deferral: Current cost parked in an asset.;;Later correction: Expense, amortisation or impairment reverses the timing benefit.|Assume ₹10 lakh routine maintenance should be expensed but is incorrectly recorded as an asset. Ignore tax.~~Current expense is understated ₹10.;;Profit and assets are overstated ₹10.;;Review invoices and asset criteria rather than relying on the account label.~~The same cash payment can produce misleading profit through classification.~~A different expenditure that genuinely improves a qualifying asset may require different treatment.|Inspect source expenditure.;;Apply the relevant recognition rule.;;Reconcile asset additions and subsequent consumption.
T10.05|Provisions and allowances rely on estimates, creating scope for both legitimate updates and opportunistic profit timing. Excessive earlier charges can create reserves later released into earnings; inadequate provisions can postpone losses. The underlying obligation or exposure must support the change.~~Review opening balance, additions, utilisation, reversals and closing balance. A release is not customer cash or an improvement in core sales by itself.|Estimate increase: Higher expected obligation or loss.;;Utilisation: Settlement or write-off against an existing balance.;;Release: Reduction when a previously estimated requirement falls.|A provision opens at ₹12 lakh. ₹3 is used to settle claims and ₹5 is released, with no additions.~~Closing provision = ₹12 − ₹3 − ₹5 = ₹4.;;The ₹3 use is distinct from the ₹5 earnings benefit, under the simplified treatment.;;Ask what new evidence supports the release.~~Provision movements can materially affect earnings without equivalent new operating revenue.~~A release can be justified; unexplained timing is a warning rather than a conclusion.|Reconcile movements.;;Inspect supporting estimates and evidence.;;Separate settlement cash from earnings releases.
T10.06|Accrual quality evaluates the relationship between accounting estimates, operating balances and realised cash. Large accruals can reflect growth or legitimate timing, but persistent unexplained gaps may indicate poor collection, estimation errors or aggressive recognition.~~Analytical accrual measures have different definitions. CFO-based and balance-sheet-based approaches can diverge around acquisitions, foreign exchange and classification changes; use coherent data and multiple years.|Working-capital accruals: Operating-balance timing differences.;;Non-cash estimates: Depreciation, provisions and related measures.;;Aggregate accrual measure: Defined gap between earnings and cash.|Net income is ₹30 lakh and CFO ₹8. Assume the chosen accrual measure is net income minus CFO.~~Defined accrual amount = ₹22.;;Trace whether receivables, inventory or non-cash items explain it.;;Compare subsequent collections and several periods before judging quality.~~The large gap identifies where further investigation is needed.~~It does not automatically establish misstatement because the two measures have different economic scopes.|State the accrual definition.;;Reconcile acquisitions and non-operating changes.;;Test later cash realisation and estimate accuracy.
T10.07|Receivables growing faster than sales can signal extended credit, collection problems or timing effects. Inventory growing faster than demand can signal overproduction, obsolescence or planned stocking. Examine age, composition, write-downs and subsequent turnover.~~Ratio deterioration is evidence of a changed pattern, not proof of misconduct. Business expansion, product launches and seasonal stocking can produce legitimate temporary increases.|Receivable warning: Longer or less reliable collections.;;Inventory warning: Slow movement or valuation risk.;;Allowance adequacy: Estimated loss or write-down versus exposure quality.|Sales rise 10%, receivables 50% and overdue balances double.~~Calculate and compare growth rates.;;Review customer terms and ageing rather than only total balances.;;Inspect subsequent receipts and credit-loss assumptions.~~The pattern warrants a collection and recognition investigation.~~A few large legitimate invoices near year-end may explain part of the gap; evidence should distinguish timing from deterioration.|Analyse age and concentration.;;Match balances with activity.;;Check subsequent collections, sales and valuation adjustments.
T10.08|Cash-flow classification can make operating cash appear stronger without changing total cash. Moving operating payments into investment or financing categories, or presenting financing-like arrangements as ordinary supplier credit, can distort analysis if inconsistent with governing rules.~~Classification depends on framework and facts. Analysts should reconcile total cash and transaction substance rather than assume every non-operating label is improper.|Operating classification: Cash from defined operating activities.;;Investing classification: Cash from eligible resource acquisition or disposal.;;Financing classification: Changes in funding claims under the framework.|Assume a ₹10 lakh payment should be operating but is incorrectly placed in investing.~~Reported CFO is overstated ₹10.;;Reported investing cash outflow is overstated ₹10.;;Total cash change remains unchanged.~~A classification error can improve a headline without creating cash.~~Evaluate the relevant reporting requirements before asserting that a particular transaction is misclassified.|Trace transaction substance.;;Reconcile all cash-flow categories.;;Read framework rules and financing arrangements.
T10.09|Related-party dealings can shift resources, risk or profit within a controlled network. Fund diversion concerns resources used contrary to the reporting entity's legitimate interests or authority. Investigation examines terms, purpose, approvals, counterparties and recoverability.~~Connections alone do not establish wrongdoing. Legitimate intercompany funding can support operations; weak documentation or unexplained transfers require closer scrutiny.|Trade arrangement: Related-party sales or purchases.;;Funding support: Loans, advances and guarantees.;;Potential diversion: Unsupported use of resources or unfair transfer.|A firm advances ₹15 lakh to a connected entity without a repayment schedule while borrowing externally at 12%.~~Identify the recipient and approvals.;;Estimate annual financing burden of ₹1.8 under the simplified assumption.;;Test business purpose, recovery evidence and required disclosures.~~The advance can impose a real cost even if no immediate accounting loss appears.~~A fair conclusion requires contract and governance evidence.|Trace beneficiaries and cash.;;Compare terms and approvals.;;Assess recovery and minority-owner effects.
T10.10|Hidden leverage includes obligations not obvious in headline debt, such as guarantees, financing-like supplier arrangements, lease commitments or pledged assets. Some are recognised liabilities; others are contingent exposures whose cash impact depends on future events.~~Additions should be scenario-based and avoid double counting. A guarantee is not always an immediate debt payment, but its risk can matter when the guaranteed party weakens.|Recognised funding debt: Reported borrowings and eligible related claims.;;Contingent guarantee: Payment exposure if specified conditions occur.;;Restricted or pledged assets: Reduced liquidity available to general creditors.|A company reports ₹40 lakh debt and guarantees ₹30 of an affiliate's debt. Assume the affiliate defaults and the full guarantee is enforceable.~~Base reported borrowing remains ₹40 before guarantee recognition effects.;;The selected stress creates ₹30 additional cash exposure.;;Assess available cash, recovery rights and related collateral.~~Headline debt can understate stressed payment needs.~~Actual guarantee limits, triggering conditions and overlap determine the exposure.|Read commitments and security notes.;;Model trigger scenarios.;;Avoid treating all contingent amounts as certain debt or ignoring them entirely.
T10.11|An audit opinion communicates the auditor's conclusion within the scope of the engagement. Unmodified, qualified, adverse and disclaimer outcomes have different meanings. An emphasis-of-matter paragraph highlights specified matters without by itself modifying the opinion.~~Audit assurance is not a prediction of investment performance or a guarantee that every error or fraud is detected. Read the exact wording, basis and scope rather than using a simple pass/fail badge.|Qualified opinion: Specific material issue with limited pervasiveness under the audit framework.;;Adverse opinion: Material and pervasive misstatement conclusion.;;Disclaimer: Insufficient basis to express an opinion.;;Emphasis of matter: Highlighted disclosed matter without that label alone modifying the opinion.|An auditor cannot obtain evidence for ₹20 lakh inventory in a ₹200 balance sheet.~~Read the scope limitation and affected statements.;;Determine the actual opinion and whether the issue is material and pervasive.;;Investigate inventory existence and valuation rather than assuming the remaining accounts validate it.~~The wording identifies the assurance limitation.~~Opinion selection depends on the audit facts and standards; the numbers alone do not determine it.|Read opinion and basis together.;;Distinguish highlighted matters from modifications.;;Investigate the affected financial consequences.
T10.12|A restatement revises previously issued information under the applicable correction process. Internal-control weaknesses indicate failures in systems intended to prevent or detect errors. Both require examining cause, size, affected periods and remediation rather than assuming an isolated event.~~A correction may improve transparency, yet repeated failures can reduce confidence in forecasts and controls. Evaluate governance response and whether similar accounts remain exposed.|Restatement: Previously reported figures corrected.;;Control weakness: Deficient design or operation of a control.;;Remediation: Evidence-backed steps to address the root cause.|A company revises prior profit from ₹25 to ₹15 lakh after finding omitted expenses.~~The correction reduces prior profit by ₹10.;;Trace related liabilities, tax and opening equity changes.;;Assess whether approval and cutoff controls were strengthened and tested.~~The earnings history and future reliability assessment both change.~~A management promise of improvement is weaker evidence than documented operation of revised controls.|Quantify all affected statements.;;Identify root causes and periods.;;Assess independent remediation evidence.
T10.13|Distress and fraud-screening models combine financial indicators to flag elevated risk. Their scores depend on population, data quality, calibration and thresholds. A distress score estimates financial vulnerability; a manipulation screen identifies unusual reporting patterns. Neither is a factual verdict about an individual company.~~Models can misclassify firms outside their training context, especially financial institutions, startups or structurally unusual businesses. Use screens to prioritise investigation.|Distress screen: Financial failure vulnerability.;;Reporting-manipulation screen: Unusual accounting patterns.;;Credit model: Default assessment for a defined population.|A screen flags rapid receivable growth and declining margins.~~Verify the underlying ratios and data dates.;;Review explanations such as acquisitions, credit terms and demand shifts.;;Use documents and cash evidence to test the concern.~~The flag determines a research priority, not an accusation.~~Thresholds and estimated probabilities require validation in the relevant industry and period.|Understand model scope.;;Validate data and comparability.;;Treat results as hypotheses requiring evidence.
T10.14|Alternative performance metrics can clarify economics when definitions and reconciliations are transparent. They can mislead when recurring costs are repeatedly excluded, favourable and unfavourable items are treated asymmetrically or definitions change. Non-cash does not mean economically costless.~~Compare adjusted earnings with reported results and cash, including dilution, reinvestment and tax. An improvement caused by a new exclusion should not be presented as operating progress without explanation.|Non-GAAP or alternative metric: Defined measure outside standard statement presentation.;;Reconciliation: Bridge to reported figures.;;Recurring exclusion: Cost repeatedly removed from the headline.|Reported profit is ₹50 lakh; management excludes ₹10 compensation and ₹8 annual restructuring to report ₹68.~~Reconcile the ₹18 additions.;;Examine whether restructuring occurs every year.;;Assess compensation's dilution and economic cost before excluding it from valuation.~~A higher adjusted number does not prove higher sustainable value.~~Disclosure requirements vary; analytical scrutiny applies regardless of the label.|Demand definitions and bridges.;;Track consistency over time.;;Assess recurrence, cash and economic cost.
T10.15|Footnotes reveal policies, estimates, movements, commitments, related parties and risks that a statement headline compresses. Investigation connects a suspicious pattern with the relevant supporting schedules and tests whether the narrative reconciles to the numbers.~~Read notes systematically rather than hunting only for alarming phrases. Omitted detail can be a limitation of disclosure rather than evidence that the most adverse explanation is true.|Policy note: How recognition and measurement work.;;Movement schedule: Why an opening balance becomes closing balance.;;Risk or commitment note: Possible future burdens and constraints.|Receivables rise ₹25 lakh while sales grow modestly.~~Read credit terms, ageing and allowance movements.;;Check acquisitions, foreign exchange and related-party balances.;;Compare subsequent collection evidence with management's explanation.~~The note analysis separates plausible causes of one balance change.~~A useful conclusion states both supporting evidence and unresolved gaps instead of treating absence of evidence as certainty.|Start from a defined question.;;Reconcile notes and statements.;;Document evidence, alternative explanations and limits.
